"Positions business units by market growth and relative market share to support portfolio allocation discussions.\n\nUse when: Review a portfolio whose units have meaningful comparable market definitions. Discuss different investment and cash-generation roles.\n\nAvoid when: Market share is a poor proxy for advantage or the units lack comparable boundaries.\n\nVisual grammar: Axes show market growth and relative share with stated thresholds. Quadrants represent the framework's distinct portfolio positions. Symbols identify units; any size encoding is declared separately.\n\nBuild: Define the relevant market for every business unit. Calculate relative share and market growth for the same period. Place units and discuss evidence beyond their quadrant before allocation.\n\nCheck: Relative share uses the stated competitor comparison. Growth rates and thresholds use a common period. Recommended actions account for economics and strategic relationships.\n\nPitfalls: Treating a quadrant nickname as an automatic divestment instruction. Using incomparable market definitions to make a unit appear stronger.\n\nExample: A diversified manufacturer compares its fast-growing niche unit with mature established lines before discussing investment and cash needs.\n\nAlternatives: GE–McKinsey matrix, Ansoff matrix."
